{{ template "chart.description" . }}

Edit this README's source in `README.md.gotmpl`, then regenerate from the
repository root with `helm-docs --chart-search-root charts/ --skip-version-footer`.

Single-instance pilot of the existing AgentOS backend. This does not deploy the
frontend, Keycloak, model services or either MCP server. It does not implement
the PostgreSQL/shared-session architecture or the US0 business audit trail.

## Values and prerequisites

The defaults in [values.yaml](values.yaml) are placeholders, not a configured
production environment. Set environment-specific overrides before installing:

| Value | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `image.repository`, `image.tag` | A published image accessible to the cluster; no `latest` tag. |
| `imagePullSecrets` | Existing registry pull Secrets, as a list of `{name: ...}` objects. |
| `config` | Non-sensitive application environment variables; every value must be a string. |
| `secrets.existingSecret` | Existing Secret in the release namespace. The chart never creates or reads its contents. |
| `secrets.azureOpenaiApiKeyKey` | Required API-key entry in that Secret. |
| `secrets.keycloakClientSecretKey` | Optional client-secret entry; leave empty for a public OIDC client. |
| `persistence.size` | Size of the chart-managed ReadWriteOnce PVC, initially 10Gi. |
| `persistence.storageClass` | `null` uses the default StorageClass; `""` explicitly disables dynamic class selection. |
| `persistence.existingClaim` | Use a dedicated existing PVC instead of creating one. |
| `ingress` | Optional HTTPS ingress; class, host, path (default `/`), TLS Secret name and controller-specific annotations. |
| `resources` | Initial requests/limits to tune against measured workload and index size. |

Do not put API keys or client secrets in `config`, a values file or Helm command
arguments. Provision the referenced Secret using your approved secret-management
process. TLS and image-pull Secrets must also exist in the release namespace.

Set the real model deployments, both MCP URLs, Keycloak issuer/client/audience,
public chatbot URL, trusted frontend URL and exact JSON-array CORS origins
under `config`. Optional settings such as `AZURE_OPENAI_REASONING_EFFORT` can be
added there as strings. Keep native agent debug disabled in production because
it can include prompts and tool arguments.

The public chatbot URL must be HTTPS. With ingress enabled, it must match
`https://<ingress.host>` plus `ingress.path` (omit `/` for a root deployment);
a TLS Secret name is required. Use a same-site hostname so SameSite=Lax login
cookies work. Register `<CHAT_PUBLIC_URL>/auth/callback` in the approved Keycloak
client. Set the frontend runtime `ASSET_COPILOT_RUN_URL` to
`<CHAT_PUBLIC_URL>/agents/asset-copilot/runs` and allow that origin in its CSP.

For a shared hostname, choose a unique `ingress.path` for each release
and include that same prefix in `CHAT_PUBLIC_URL`. The backend uses native
FastAPI `root_path`; auth endpoints and cookies are scoped under the prefix.
Use backend and frontend builds containing this support, not the original
`v0.1.0` release. Existing root deployments remain supported.

The ingress routes the configured path without rewriting or StripPrefix middleware.
Reuse an existing TLS Secret when sharing the platform hostname; do not add
cert-manager annotations to manage a certificate already owned elsewhere.
Configure your actual
controller for SSE: disable response buffering and allow sufficiently long
read/write idle timeouts. For ingress-nginx, typical annotations are
`nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/proxy-buffering: "off"` and
`nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/proxy-read-timeout: "3600"`, plus the matching
`proxy-send-timeout`. Those annotations are not portable to other controllers;
none are silently applied by this chart.

Pods carry `networking/traffic-allowed: "yes"`, matching the Data Layer and
namespaces configured with an allow-whitelisted NetworkPolicy. Where such a
policy is enforced, omitting this label can block ingress traffic even while
kubelet health probes pass. DevOps must verify the target namespace's policies;
this label alone does not create a policy or guarantee connectivity. Application
authentication remains required. This chart does not create NetworkPolicies.

## Validate and install

From the repository root, with Helm 3.20.0 and Bash:

```bash
helm lint --strict charts/asset-copilot
helm template copilot charts/asset-copilot
```

After replacing the placeholder configuration, publishing the chosen image,
and provisioning Secrets/storage, an installation uses:

```bash
helm upgrade --install copilot charts/asset-copilot \
  --namespace "$NAMESPACE" --create-namespace \
  --values "$ENVIRONMENT_VALUES" --wait --timeout 10m
```

`NAMESPACE` is the target namespace and `ENVIRONMENT_VALUES` is your non-secret
environment overrides file. Rendering is not proof that referenced resources,
endpoints, permissions, a default StorageClass or a compatible ingress controller
exist. This repository's `Checks` workflow lints and packages charts, verifies
generated documentation and checks license metadata; it does not connect to a
cluster or validate rendered manifests against Kubernetes schemas.

## Persistent storage

This chart is tenant-independent. Keep environment-specific values, external
storage manifests and deployment notes outside the chart. DevOps selects the
release namespace and supplies non-secret environment overrides at installation.

By default, [templates/pvc.yaml](templates/pvc.yaml) creates a dedicated
ReadWriteOnce PVC for each release in its namespace. Configure
`persistence.size` and `persistence.storageClass` for the target cluster; no
tenant-specific PVC manifest is needed.

Alternatively, set `persistence.existingClaim` to a dedicated PVC already
provisioned in the release namespace. The chart then does not create a PVC and
ignores `persistence.storageClass` and `persistence.size`; the external PVC
controls those settings. Never share the disk with the Data Layer, another
database or another running release.

Choose storage compatible with SQLite and LanceDB, including LanceDB's atomic
safe-commit operations and the pod's fsGroup permissions. Azure Files has failed
these LanceDB operations; validate the selected driver rather than assuming any
ReadWriteOnce volume is compatible. With `WaitForFirstConsumer`, a PVC may remain
Pending until the Deployment mounts it; do not wait for it to bind before
running Helm.

When switching to a new PVC, migrate any required SQLite/index data with the
application stopped, or explicitly rebuild the index afterward. No data copying
or indexing is automatic.

## PDF knowledge upload

A rebuilt image exposes an authenticated PDF upload into the existing
`/data/knowledge` store, using the same Keycloak client as the chat; no extra
PVC, Job or configuration is needed. See the
[upload instructions](https://github.com/Cosmo-Tech/asset-agentic-app#upload-a-pdf-to-a-deployed-tenant).

## Runtime and data lifecycle

One replica and the image's one-worker command are intentional. The chart uses
`Recreate`, not a rolling update, and has no HPA. Restarts/upgrades interrupt
active chats and require login again because OAuth/session/approval guards are
process-local. A ConfigMap checksum triggers rollout after configuration changes;
after rotating an external Secret, explicitly restart the Deployment to load it.

The pod runs as UID/GID 10001 without service-account token mounting, elevated
capabilities or a writable root filesystem. `/tmp` is a bounded 128Mi memory
volume. SQLite and LanceDB share persistent `/data`; their paths are fixed to
avoid accidental ephemeral storage. The storage driver must support the pod's
fsGroup permissions. Internal pod probes use `/health`, including with a public
prefix configured, without requiring model/MCP
availability. Startup has up to five minutes; termination allows 45 seconds
for the image's 30-second Uvicorn shutdown.

The chart-managed PVC has `helm.sh/resource-policy: keep`: uninstall retains
conversation/index data and storage charges. Its lifecycle and backups remain
the operator's responsibility. Reusing retained data on another release requires
an explicit `persistence.existingClaim`; do not share a SQLite/index volume with
another running release. Do not shrink a PVC; expansion depends on StorageClass
support. Back up SQLite consistently, accounting for WAL files, and include
the knowledge index. Index approved documents explicitly using the image's
existing `/app/scripts/index_pdf_knowledge.py` command in a maintenance workload
with access to the same data and approved documents; indexing is not automatic.

